  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1216 PM MDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nSCATTERED SHOWERS AND THUNDERSTORMS TODAY: \n\nThunderstorms have been ongoing through the morning and early \nafternoon today, and will continue while moisture lingers across\nthe region and a jet streak aloft provides lift. This morning's\n300mb objectively analyzed map from the Storm Prediction Center\nshows this jet streak stretching from southern California \nthrough eastern Utah, with divergence aloft aligned with the \nsouthern side of the jet streak. Guidance shows this jet streak \nmoving across our CWA through the day as a trough pushes into \nthe West. This morning's 18Z sounding from GJT shows that \nprecipitable water remains elevated, with today's value \nexceeding the 90th percentile. Given the upper-level support and\nmoisture, showers and storms will persist through the evening, \nwith coverage becoming isolated after sunset. The primary \nconcern with this activity is heavy rainfall, which could cause \nflash flooding in localized areas, especially around newer burn \nscars and areas of susceptible terrain. Small hail and gusty \noutflow winds are also possible. \n\nGUSTY AND DRIER THROUGH FRIDAY: \n\nAfternoon wind gusts will be stronger today through Friday, \nwith the strongest forecast gusts being around 25 to 35 mph. \nThis is due to an increased pressure gradient and periods of \nstronger winds aloft as the trough moves deeper into the West. \nHowever, one caveat to consider is that if cloud cover exceeds \nwhat is forecast the magnitude of gusts could be weaker.\n\nDrier air moves in tonight which will keep precipitation \nchances pretty low through the rest of the workweek. However, \nsome isolated afternoon convection is possible over the higher \nterrain tomorrow and Friday. \n\nRETURN OF MOISTURE THIS WEEKEND: \n\nGuidance continues to favor a moisture push this weekend, with \nthe most anomalous moisture moving in on Sunday. How this \nforecast develops is largely dependent on the tropical storm \nactivity in the Pacific, but models have consistently shown this\nmoisture transport between runs. Increased moisture could allow\nfor more widespread precipitation, so be sure to stay tuned to \nthe forecast for this weekend as it develops.",
